.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility rebases one ZFS filesystem onto another.
Given two sides that diverged from a common base, it replays the changes of
one side onto the other, or reports the names it could not decide and why.
It is not a
.Xr zfs 8
verb and it is not built into the kernel: it is a userspace program that
does all of its work through the
.Sy libzfs_core
and
.Sy libzfs
libraries, execs nothing, and must run as root.
.Pp
Three trees are read, each through the
.Pa .zfs/snapshot
directory of its dataset:
.Ar base ,
the branch point;
.Ar from ,
the side whose changes are replayed; and
.Ar onto ,
the side they are replayed onto.
Only the two sides are named.
The base is derived: each side's origin chain is walked back \(em the
snapshot, then its dataset's origin, then that dataset's origin \(em to the
nearest dataset both descend from, and the older of the two snapshots they
name there, by
.Sy createtxg ,
is the base.
Two sides that share no origin are refused unless
.Fl -allow-unrelated
is given, and so is a pair whose derived base turns out to be one of the
arguments: one side already contains the other, and what is wanted there is
not a rebase.
.Pp
The three walks also give the unchanged set, which is the prune.
A pool of either side is declared to hold what
.Ar base
holds \(em and its bytes are then never read \(em when the object number,
the type, the link count, the number of names, the generation number and
the change time to the nanosecond all agree with base's, every one of its
names is in that same base pool, and the extended attributes and both ACLs
compare equal.
What the object number, the generation and the change time are trusted for
is the object's bytes and the attributes its znode keeps \(em the mode, the
owner, the group, the file flags, the size \(em since ZFS moves a
.Sy ctime
for a write and for every one of those, and an object number is only ever
reused by an object of a later generation.
The extended attributes are compared rather than trusted because one
storage escapes the
.Sy ctime :
an attribute written into an object's hidden directory
.Pq Sy xattr Ns = Ns Sy dir , and the fallback an SA write takes when it fails
never touches the object's own znode, so a
.Ar from
side whose only change was such an attribute would otherwise be dropped in
silence.
The two ACLs are compared beside them because it costs nothing.
Every field is in memory from the walks, so the prune reads nothing from
disk and asks ZFS nothing; the OpenZFS source line for each of those facts
is listed above
.Fn zr_oracle_prune
in
.Pa src/yellow.h .
.Fl -allow-unrelated
turns the prune off, because an object number in one lineage means nothing
in another.
.Pp
The decision is made over names, over hardlink pools \(em a pool being one
file and every name it has \(em and over content.
A name only one side touched takes that side's version; a pool edited
through one of its names is edited for every name of it; a name both sides
changed differently, a pool the two sides disagree about, a split one side
healed and an addition whose every anchor the other side deleted are
conflicts, and a conflict stops the names it covers and nothing else.
.Pp
The one output is a manifest.
It is a single document in two sections: a scoped walk of the result
namespace, in which every name with something to say carries an action \(em
.Cm rm ,
.Cm ln Ar path ,
.Cm cp Ar path ,
.Cm dup Ar path ,
.Cm write Ar path
or
.Cm conflict Ar n
\(em and, after it, one record per conflict giving its class, a one-sentence
why, and the pools of that group in each of the three trees.
A header names the rebase.
Above its
.Cm #mode
line is the run: the result, the form, the three snapshots the run actually
read with the guid of each beside its name, the pre-apply snapshot and the
properties to put back in the dataset form, which side the tool snapshotted
itself, the hold tag, how the conflicts were answered in advance and when the
manifest was written.
From
.Cm #mode
on is the decision: the mode and the counts of actions and conflicts.
The version is 5, and no other is read.
Names are escaped as a backslash and three octal digits wherever they are
not plain printable ASCII.
Every name in the manifest, in the resolution and in the shared name table
is the tree's own \(em relative to the root of the tree, written with a
leading slash, and never a path on the running system.
Where the result happens to be mounted is the run's business and no
document's: no mount prefix reaches the engine or either document.
The format is a document of its own,
.Pa v4-manifest.md
in the project's notes, and it is the contract between this tool and
whatever applies or answers it.
.Pp
The manifest is then applied.
.Fl -onto
decides where:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy The clone form ,
.Fl -onto
given a snapshot.
.Fl -result
names a new dataset, created as a read-only clone of that snapshot with its
.Sy mountpoint
property
.Sy none ,
and mounted at the run's own private directory, which is the only place it
is ever mounted while the rebase is open.
The rebase is made in it and nothing of yours is written to at all.
At done the clone is handed to the void: unmounted, read-only, its
.Sy mountpoint
still
.Sy none ,
and the tool says how to place it, because where a rebased tree belongs is
yours to say and not the tool's.
.It
.Sy The dataset form ,
.Fl -onto
given a dataset.
The rebase is made in that dataset itself, and
.Fl -result
names the snapshot the tool takes of it before anything is applied, which
is kept afterwards as the before-image.
Exclusivity is the unmount: the dataset is unmounted from its own
mountpoint and mounted at the run's private directory instead, with its
.Sy mountpoint
property untouched.
A dataset somebody is using will not unmount, and then the tool says so and
exits 2.
Nothing is ever forced.
The take also puts
.Sy readonly
to off and
.Sy canmount
to
.Sy noauto ,
both of them while the dataset is off its mountpoint, and the manifest's
header remembers what each of them was.
The private mount is root's alone and writable for its whole life.
The dataset then stays there for the whole of the rebase, the
.Sy conflicts
gate included, and is handed home exactly twice: at done and at
.Fl -abort ,
off the private mount with both properties put back and mounted where its
.Sy mountpoint
property says, which is untouched throughout.
A rebase waiting for its conflicts to be answered is a half rebased tree,
and a half rebased tree is not put back into service.
A caught signal at a gate leaves it privately mounted, as a hard kill does,
and the next verb takes it from there.
.Pp
That hand-back is the first thing done and
.Fl -abort
do and not the last.
Both give a rebase back in the order a run took it in, reversed, with the
bookkeeping last: the walks closed, the result put back into service and
asked whether it is where its
.Sy mountpoint
property says, then the holds released, the record taken off, the snapshots
the rebase owned destroyed and the run directory removed.
Nothing is released until the tree is back where it belongs, and the record
\(em the only thing that names the manifest, and through it the tag, the
pre-apply snapshot and the two property values \(em is the last of it to go.
.Pp
An unmount refuses while anybody holds the mount: an open file, a working
directory, a child mount.
At the
.Sy conflicts
gate a person is expected to be working inside the private mount, so a
shell left there is the ordinary case.
Nothing after the unmount is then done: the record and the holds stay, the
run directory stays, the mount path and the reason are printed, and the
exit is 3.
The next
.Fl -continue
or
.Fl -abort
finishes the settle once the mount is free.
done never blocks on drift; this is the one thing it does block on, because
giving the dataset back is the promise the dataset form makes.
A dataset whose
.Sy canmount
is
.Sy off
has no home to be handed back to and is refused at precondition with
exit 2.
.El
.Pp
A reboot in the middle of a rebase leaves the result unmounted in either
form: nothing at boot mounts a dataset whose
.Sy mountpoint
is
.Sy none ,
and nothing mounts one whose
.Sy canmount
is
.Sy noauto .
So a half rebased tree is never in service after a reboot either, and the
next
.Fl -continue
or
.Fl -abort
mounts it privately again from there.
.Pp
Special files are carried like everything else.
A fifo or a device node is made with
.Fn mkfifoat
or
.Fn mknodat ,
and a unix-domain socket the one way one can be made, by binding one;
the mode, the owner, the times and the rest are written
afterwards as for any other object, and an address too long for a socket
is refused in words rather than truncated.
On
.Fx
the bind is
.Fn bindat ,
which takes the parent's descriptor, so only the leaf name is measured
and the depth of the run directory is out of it.
Elsewhere it is
.Fn bind
at the root's path with the action's appended, both lengths are in the
measurement, and the refusal says which of the two is at fault: the path
in the tree, which no root would make room for, or the root this result
happens to be written at.
An object already standing where an action must act, and carrying an
immutable, append-only or no-unlink flag of either the system or the user
family, has those flags taken off before it is removed, rewritten or given
new attributes, and what the decision asks for goes back on last: ZFS
refuses the unlink, the write and the attribute change alike on such an
object, so an apply that did not clear them would stop part way through the
tree.
Above securelevel 0 the system three cannot be cleared at all, so two
kinds of object are refused at precondition with exit 2, naming the first
such name and the side it is on, before anything is touched.
One of
.Ar onto Ns 's
carrying
.Sy schg ,
.Sy sappnd
or
.Sy sunlnk
at a name the decision would remove, rewrite or re-pool, which the apply
would have to unlock and could not.
And one of
.Ar from Ns 's
carrying one of them that the decision would write into the result, which
the apply would lock by writing the flag on, and which a
.Fl -continue
redoing the action, or the applying1 self-check putting it back, would
then be unable to touch.
The user three
.Pq Sy uchg , Sy uappnd , Sy uunlnk
are not in that guard: they come off for the owner at any securelevel, and
ZFS refuses to set them at all.
.Pp
A side given as a dataset rather than a snapshot is snapshotted by the tool
under a generated name, recorded as tool-made, and destroyed when the
rebase ends, at done or at
.Fl -abort .
If that snapshot were wanted, a snapshot would have been passed.
.Pp
A rebase outlives the process that started it.
What makes it one thing rather than a process's leavings is the record it
writes on the result, the holds it takes on its inputs, and the gates it
passes; see
.Sx THE RECORD ,
.Sx HOLDS
and
.Sx THE GATES .
.Sh OPTIONS
Every flag has a long form and a short form, and the two are the same
flag: the letters are given with the words below and are the letters
the usage text and
.Pa README.md
carry.
.Fl -off-of
is another spelling of
.Fl -from
and
.Fl -to
of
.Fl -onto ,
neither with a letter of its own, and the three test aids at the end
of this section are long only.
A long form takes its value as
.Fl -from Ar snapshot
or as
.Fl -from Ns = Ns Ar snapshot ;
a short form takes the argument after it, and short flags do not
bundle.
.Pp
The inputs and the form of the run:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl f , Fl -from Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
The side whose changes are replayed.
Spelled
.Fl -off-of
as well.
A dataset here is snapshotted by the tool.
.It Fl t , Fl -onto Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
The side they are replayed onto, and the form of the run: a snapshot is
cloned as
.Fl -result ,
a dataset is rebased in place.
Spelled
.Fl -to
as well.
.It Fl r , Fl -result Ar name
Two meanings, one per form.
With
.Fl -onto
a snapshot it is the dataset the rebased clone is created as.
With
.Fl -onto
a dataset it is the name of the pre-apply snapshot taken of that dataset \(em
the short name after the
.Sy @ ,
or a full name whose dataset part is onto.
It is a start's flag and nothing else: a verb names its rebase with
.Ar ident ,
the one operand, and
.Fl -result
beside one is a usage error that says so
.Pq ruled 2026-09-08 .
.It Fl u , Fl -allow-unrelated
Rebase two sides that share no origin.
The base is not derived; it is
.Fl -base ,
which this flag needs.
Pruning is turned off with it, because object numbers mean nothing across
lineages.
.It Fl b , Fl -base Ar snapshot
Only with
.Fl -allow-unrelated ,
and that flag takes no other answer: the tree to treat as the branch
point.
Its
.Sy createtxg
must be at or below that of both inputs.
Reading the two sides against the empty tree where neither a derived nor
a given base was there is gone
.Pq ruled 2026-09-06 :
it made every name of either side an add and every disagreement a
conflict, which is a decision about two trees that were never compared.
.It Ar ident
The one operand, and the only argument of this tool that is not a flag:
the name of the rebase a verb acts on.
It is resolved in five steps, the first that matches winning:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
an absolute path: the manifest at it.
A path with no file at it is refused there and never looked for as a
name.
.It
a dataset carrying the record whose name is
.Ar ident
or ends in
.Sy /
and it: the clone form's result, in full or by the last part of its
name.
.It
a snapshot of a dataset carrying the record whose name after the
.Sy @
is
.Ar ident ,
or the whole snapshot spelled out: the dataset form's pre-apply
snapshot.
.It
a relative path to a manifest.
.It
a run directory,
.Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/ Ns Ar ident ,
which is what a crash before the record leaves and what
.Fl -abort
removes.
.El
.Pp
Steps 2 and 3 search every imported pool.
Two rebases that answer to one
.Ar ident
are refused with every match printed, and never chosen between.
A path names its rebase through the header \(em
.Cm #result
in the clone form, and the dataset of
.Cm #onto
in the dataset form, where
.Cm #result
is the pre-apply snapshot as
.Fl -result
spelled it \(em and is resolved with
.Xr realpath 3
first, because that is what the record keeps.
Whatever step matched, the two halves are then held against each other:
the record's
.Sy zfs_rebase:manifest
must be the file and that file's header must name this result back, and
a mismatch is refused with exit 2, saying both sides.
The identifier is held against ZFS's own naming rule before it is ever
made into a path, and as what it is spelled as: a word with an
.Sy @
in it is held against the snapshot rule, so that a well-formed snapshot
name that names no rebase is refused as that rather than as no name at
all.
A start writes a manifest and reads none, so it takes no operand at all.
.El
.Pp
The flags:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Dry run.
The manifest is written and nothing else: to the file
.Fl o
names, which is then the user's and stays, or to standard output when
there is none \(em and nothing else of a dry run goes there \(em with no
clone, no record, no holds, no resolution, and
.Fl -result
ignored.
It is the run leaving before the
.Sy applying1
gate, which is also the path a caught signal takes there.
Where a side was given as a dataset the tool still snapshots it, because
there has to be something to read, and destroys that snapshot again before
it exits.
.It Fl p , Fl -permissive-merge
Permissive-merge mode, which decides what a hard link made on one side
means when the other side had its own opinion about the same file.
Strict, the default, treats the link as a claim to be held against the
other side and raises a conflict; permissive treats a link as a link and
follows it.
Two shapes differ between the modes.
Where one side linked a name into one pool and the other side linked the
same name into another, following both links joins the two pools into one,
which neither side asked for: strict raises an
.Sy unexpressed-sharing
conflict, permissive joins them.
Where one side made a name a link of another file and the other side
edited that file, strict raises a
.Sy disagree
conflict, since the linked name defends the content it had in base;
permissive reads the link as "this name is that file from now on" and the
name adopts whatever the file ends up as.
An adoption that meets an edit of the adopted name, two sides adopting one
name into files whose bytes differ, and a cycle of adoptions are conflicts
in both modes.
Use strict when a link on the from side may be accidental or every link
that crosses an edit should be looked at, and permissive when the from
side's links are deliberate deduplication or aliasing that should stick.
The mode is written into the manifest's header as
.Cm #mode
and every verb of the rebase runs under it.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Report counts and the steps taken on standard error.
.It Fl o , Fl -manifest Ar file
Write the manifest to
.Ar file
rather than into the run directory, and the resolution to
.Ar file Ns .resolution
beside it.
The record names the manifest, and the resolution is beside it by rule.
The files are yours: the tool records them and does not remove them, where
its own two go with the run.
It is a start option, and a dry run's.
On a verb it is a usage error, as
.Fl q
is: the start chose where the manifest goes, the record names the path
from then on and
.Sy done
acts on it, so there is no later moment at which the choice could be
made.
.It Fl O , Fl -take-onto
Write the resolution's skeleton with every conflict answered
.Sy onto
rather than unanswered, and record that this is what was asked for.
The document is then complete from its first byte, so the run passes its
own
.Sy conflicts
gate and carries on to
.Sy done
unless
.Fl -no-merge
holds it; and a later
.Fl -restart ,
which puts the resolution back to its skeleton, puts back the skeleton
this flag asked for and not an unanswered one.
It belongs to a fresh run: no verb writes a skeleton, and giving it to one
is a usage error.
.It Fl F , Fl -take-from
The same, answering every conflict
.Sy from .
The two exclude each other.
.It Fl i , Fl -interactive Op Ar command
Open a child on the resolution at the
.Sy conflicts
gate, wait for it, and read the document back.
Without it the gate is headless, which is what a system tool does by
default: it goes on when the resolution is complete and stops when it is
not.
.Pp
.Ar command
is the command line that edits the file, and the tool never splits it: the
child is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/bin/sh -c 'command "$@"' command resolution
.Ed
.Pp
which is how git runs an editor.
The command's own flags therefore survive \(em
.Ql code --wait
is one command \(em and the resolution's path arrives as the last
argument, a positional parameter the shell never interpolates into
anything.
An editor gets that path and nothing else, and finds the manifest beside
it under the same directory.
With no
.Ar command
the built-in picker opens instead, which is given the path and the
directories the four trees are at; a build with no picker in it says so
and exits 2, which is this build.
.Pp
The value is optional, so a bare word after
.Fl i
is that command
.Em unless
it is the only bare word on a verb's line, where it is
.Ar ident :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -c -i myrun          myrun is ident, and the built-in opens
zfs_rebase -c -i nvim myrun     nvim is the command, myrun is ident
zfs_rebase -c myrun -i nvim     the same; the position does not matter
zfs_rebase -i nvim -f a -t b -r c   a start takes no ident: nvim it is
zfs_rebase -i --from a          a word with a dash is a flag, not a value
.Ed
.Pp
The attached form
.Fl -interactive Ns = Ns Ar command
always names the command, and an empty value there is a usage error.
.Pp
The child opens whenever the gate is reached, whether or not the
resolution is already complete:
.Fl -take-onto Fl i
opens it on the answered skeleton, a
.Fl -continue Fl i
that arrives with a hand-answered file opens it, and
.Fl M Fl i
opens it and then holds the gate.
A rebase whose decision declared no conflict reaches no gate and opens
nothing.
The gate's own work comes first \(em the phase written, and on a
.Fl -continue
the check whose drift lines go into the document \(em so the child sees
the resolution as it stands, and the line saying how many names are
unanswered is printed after the child and not before it.
.Pp
Exit 0 from the child, and the document is read again with every refusal
.Fl -continue
makes of one, and the gate is then decided as it always is: complete and
no
.Fl -no-merge
goes on, an unanswered name stops.
Anything else \(em a status, a signal, a command that could not be run at
all \(em and the gate stands with the file as the child last saved it,
the exit is 1, and nothing reopens the child by itself: the rebase carries
on with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -c ident -i
.Ed
.Pp
While it waits the tool ignores
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGQUIT
the way
.Xr system 3
does, so a Ctrl-C reaches the editor and not the tool, and it forwards a
.Dv SIGTERM
of its own to the child.
Where its standard input is a terminal it saves the termios before the
fork and puts them back after the wait if the child left them changed, so
that an editor that crashed cannot leave the terminal raw.
A kill of the tool while the child runs leaves the gate the same way, with
the child orphaned and finishing on its own.
.Pp
The flag is not latched: it belongs to the invocation, so a
.Fl -continue
that should open a child says it each time, and a start given it that
reaches the gate in the same process is interactive there.
Nothing about it is written into the record or the header.
.It Fl M , Fl -no-merge
Stop at the
.Sy conflicts
gate however the resolution reads, complete or not, which is what a script
that means to edit the document before the merge asks for.
The gate is left where it is, so the next
.Fl -continue
without the flag passes it.
Once the gate is passed it is an error: a
.Fl -continue
from
.Sy applying2
or from
.Sy done
is past the merge and has no gate left for the flag to hold, and it is
refused rather than run.
.It Fl V , Fl -verify
A verb and only a verb: it reports on one rebase and writes nothing
anywhere; see below.
The checks run on a schedule of their own
.Pq Sx THE CHECKS ,
so there is nothing left for the word to ask for, and beside a start,
beside
.Fl -dry-run
and beside
.Fl -continue ,
.Fl -restart
or
.Fl -abort
it is a usage error, exit 2, rather than a flag that means something else
than it used to.
.Fl -from
and
.Fl -onto
go with it as they go with any verb, checked against the header;
.Fl -result
names what a start makes, and a verb starts nothing.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Silence the final check's report.
It is a start option and is latched in
.Sy zfs_rebase:quiet
for the whole run, so that whichever invocation reaches
.Sy done
finds it there; on any other verb, and on
.Fl -dry-run ,
it is a usage error.
A dry run refuses
.Fl -take-onto ,
.Fl -take-from ,
.Fl -interactive
and
.Fl -no-merge
for the same reason: it writes a manifest and stops, so no skeleton is
written for a take flag to answer and no conflicts gate is reached for the
other two to hold \(em nothing for the flag to act on and no record to latch
it in.
It silences that one report and nothing else: the check is still made, its
verdict still stands in the exit status, and the report of the conflicts
gate and of the
.Fl -verify
verb are printed as always.
.El
.Pp
The verbs work on a rebase that already exists.
Each names its run with
.Ar ident ,
its one operand, and takes
.Fl v
and the two sides;
.Fl -continue
takes the flags of the gate as well, and nothing else is theirs: no two of
them go together, and every one keys on the record and on the manifest it
names, so that a dataset carrying no record is refused untouched.
Each also checks that every snapshot the header names is still the snapshot
it named, by guid, since a snapshot destroyed and taken again under the
same name is another snapshot.
.Pp
.Fl -from
and
.Fl -onto
are optional on all four and change nothing: a verb reads the two sides out
of the header.
What they do is say which rebase the person thinks this is, and each is
checked against the header by name and by guid \(em a name is what a
snapshot is called and the guid is what it is \(em with both numbers
printed on a mismatch, which is exit 2 with nothing touched.
One of them or both stand beside
.Ar ident ;
.Fl -result
is what a start calls the thing it makes, and beside any verb it is
refused, exit 2, with a line saying what a verb takes instead.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c , Fl -continue
Take the rebase on from the gate its record names, through the gates that
are left, in one process.
Applying is idempotent \(em every action means "make this true" \(em so what is
already true is left alone and what is not is made, which is why a fresh
run, a resumed one and a repair are one code path.
It checks at every gate it passes, under no flag
.Pq Sx THE CHECKS ,
and prints what it found: how every action of
the document stands, how every answered name of the resolution stands
against the side it names, and what the result holds outside them both.
It writes nothing into the tree, and the only document it writes is the
resolution: at the
.Sy conflicts
gate, where the drift it found becomes lines with the choice
.Sy keep ,
and at the done gate, where what the last check found is written with the
choice
.Sy \-
as the record of it.
What mends a result is the
.Sy applying1
stage's own self-check, which redoes any clean action that did not land and
puts back the names no action speaks for, with or without the flag;
conflicted names are never touched, because no action names one.
.It Fl R , Fl -restart
Put the result back as onto was \(em the clone destroyed and made again from
the onto snapshot the header names, with the same record, or the dataset
rolled back to the pre-apply snapshot it names \(em and apply the recorded
manifest from the first gate, with the resolution put back to its
skeleton.
Nothing is decided again: the manifest is the decision.
A resolution's edits are discarded by definition.
.It Fl A , Fl -abort
Put the result back first \(em the clone destroyed, or the dataset rolled
back to its pre-apply snapshot and mounted where it belongs again with
.Sy readonly
and
.Sy canmount
as the header kept them \(em and only then release the holds, destroy the
snapshots the rebase owned, take every one of the result's
.Sy zfs_rebase
properties off it, unlink the two documents the run wrote into its own
directory, and remove that directory.
As if the run never happened.
A pair
.Fl o
named is the exception and stays where it was asked for, here exactly as
at done: the tool removes no file outside the run directory.
.Pp
It stops where a step refuses and does nothing after it, so that what is
left is a rebase a second
.Fl -abort
can find: a rollback that cannot be made \(em the snapshot gone, or a newer
one in the way \(em and a private mount somebody is standing in both leave
the record, the holds and the documents as they were, with the reason
printed and a non-zero exit.
Which of those it does is the manifest's to say, and that file is there at
every gate, since the run writes its header before its record.
Where it has been lost all the same, removed by a hand,
.Fl -abort
gives the holds back by walking the result's pool for the record's tag,
destroys the snapshot the run took for itself where it took one, which
that walk finds held under the tag and named with it, undoes the private
mount and takes the record off, and then says what it cannot do without
the manifest: the form, the pre-apply snapshot and the two property values
went with the file, so it
destroys nothing else and rolls nothing back, and it cannot put
.Sy readonly
or
.Sy canmount
back, for which it prints the two
.Ic zfs set
commands.
A result with no record at all but a run directory of its own, and a result
that is not there at all with its run directory still standing, are the
other half of this, and what the directory holds says which of three cases
it is.
A directory with no manifest in it is the window before the first write: it
is removed, and saying so is all there is to say.
A birth manifest \(em a header with
.Sy #actions 0
and
.Sy #conflicts 0
\(em is the window between that write and the record: nothing was held and
nothing was written to the result, so what goes with the directory is the
snapshot the tool took of
.Ar from
and, because this window proves the run never reached a record, the dataset
form's pre-apply snapshot as well.
A decision manifest is a rebase whose result was destroyed under it \(em a
.Fl -restart
whose second clone failed, or a
.Ic zfs destroy
of the result by hand \(em where the record that named the tag went with
the dataset and the holds under it could be reached by nothing else: the
tag is released on the three snapshots the header names, the tool's own
snapshot is destroyed, the documents are unlinked and the directory is
removed.
The pre-apply snapshot is named and left in that last case, since a rebase
that lost its result and a done whose unlink failed have the same shape and
one of them means to keep it.
A manifest that will not parse is the one shape
.Fl -abort
refuses, since a file that says a rebase was here and cannot be read is not
a directory to remove quietly: it releases nothing, removes nothing, says
so and exits 2.
Step 5 of the identifier is how a name reaches that directory when no
dataset carries a record under it; the other three verbs say there is no
rebase to move and name
.Fl -abort .
The one thing it can still read is the
.Sy mountpoint
property, which the two forms never share: a path is a dataset of yours and
is mounted at it, and
.Sy none
is a clone of the tool's and is left unmounted for you to destroy or to
place.
It prints the command for each form and leaves the choice to you.
.It Fl V , Fl -verify
Report and write nothing at all, so that a deliberate
edit to a rebased file is shown and never overwritten: it writes
nothing to the tree and moves nothing that is where it should be.
It reports on a rebase that is open and on one that is over; which of the
two this is, the record says, and a result whose rebase reached done carries
none.
Every action of the manifest is classified as
.Sy done ,
.Sy pending
(the object is still as onto had it),
.Sy blocked
(impossible until a conflict beneath it is resolved),
.Sy drifted
(it matches neither the manifest's outcome nor onto's original) or
.Sy unchecked
(a tree it would have to be read against could not be found), and the
report gives each outcome's count and first name.
After them come the names no action spoke for, whose expected object is the
one onto had there, one line per kind with its count and first name:
.Sy gone
(onto had it and the result does not),
.Sy extra
(the result has it and nothing expected it),
.Sy changed
(there, but not the object onto had) and
.Sy unpooled
(there and equal, but no longer one object with the names it shared onto's
with).
With
.Fl v
the whole list follows.
.Pp
The result is read where it is mounted, which for a rebase in flight is the
run's private mount, where the rule keeps it.
Where it is mounted nowhere \(em what a reboot leaves, and what a hand that
unmounted it leaves \(em the check mounts it at the run directory's
.Pa mnt
with
.Fn zfs_mount_at
for the length of the read and unmounts it again, leaving the run directory
to the run: the next verb takes the rebase from there.
It sets
.Sy readonly
on nothing in either form, so a result a kill left writable inside a stage
is still writable after the report and one outside a stage is still
read-only.
.Pp
A settled result carries no record, so its name answers to no step of the
identifier: the tool says it is not a zfs_rebase result and asks for the
manifest instead.
The manifest is what names a settled rebase, and only a
.Fl -manifest
one can: the manifest a run writes for itself is unlinked at done with the
run directory.
Given its path, the verb makes the final check over again, with no gate to
make it at.
It wants both documents \(em the manifest and the resolution beside it \(em
since the expected tree is onto's names with the manifest's actions and that
resolution's choices applied, and a resolution that is not there or cannot
be read is exit 2 saying so.
The inputs are the header's: each of
.Sy base ,
.Sy from
and
.Sy onto
is looked up by the name the header kept, its guid must be the guid the
header kept, and a name that is gone or that another snapshot wears now is
exit 2 naming the snapshot and printing both numbers.
No pool is searched, as it is on a rebase in flight: a settled rebase is
checked against the identity it wrote down, or not at all.
Where a side was given as a dataset,
.Sy #made
says the tool took that snapshot itself and
.Sy done
destroyed it; that one is gone by design, so the check covers the names and
onto's bytes, says which actions it leaves
.Sy unchecked ,
and exits 0 all the same.
The result is read wherever it is mounted \(em a dataset is at home, and so
is a clone you have placed \(em and a settled clone in the void is mounted
nowhere, so the check mounts it at the run directory's
.Pa mnt
with
.Fn zfs_mount_at
for the length of the read and takes that mount and the directory away
again, whatever it found.
No property of the result is touched either way, nothing is fixed, and the
exit status is 0 clean and 3 with drift, by the same rule as every other
check.
.El
.Pp
Three test aids exist for the project's own harness and are not part of
ordinary use:
.Fl -posix
runs the whole decision over three plain directories given as
.Ar basedir fromdir ontodir
and writes the manifest, which is how the core is exercised where there is
no ZFS;
.Fl -build-fixture
materializes a fixture's
.Pa base ,
.Pa from ,
.Pa onto
and
.Pa expect
under a directory; and
.Fl -edit-fixture
turns a directory already holding one of those trees into another of them
by the smallest set of edits, so that everything the two agree on keeps its
inode and its ctime.
.Sh THE RECORD
While a rebase is open the result carries four user properties, written by
the create itself in the clone form and set on the dataset before anything
is touched in the other.
User properties inherit down the naming tree, so every one of them is set
locally and read back as a local value only: an inherited value is no
record of ours.
.Bl -tag -width "zfs_rebase:manifest" -compact
.It Sy zfs_rebase:phase
the last gate the run passed:
.Sy decided ,
.Sy applying1 ,
.Sy conflicts
or
.Sy applying2
.It Sy zfs_rebase:manifest
the absolute path the manifest was written to
.It Sy zfs_rebase:tag
the tag its holds are filed under
.It Sy zfs_rebase:quiet
.Sy yes ,
where the start was given
.Fl -quiet
.El
.Pp
Everything else about the rebase is in the header of the manifest that
path names: the three snapshots with their guids, the form, the mode,
which side the tool snapshotted itself, how the skeleton was answered, and
in the dataset form the pre-apply snapshot and the
.Sy readonly
and
.Sy canmount
values to give back.
Either document names the run \(em the property points at the manifest and
the header names the result \(em and nothing else is ever written to a
dataset by this tool.
.Pp
The file that path names exists from the record's first instant: the run
writes the whole header, with
.Sy #actions 0
and
.Sy #conflicts 0
and no body, before it writes the record, and writes the decision over it
when it has one.
So the order a run acquires things in is the pre-apply snapshot in the
dataset form, the run directory, that header, the record, the holds, the
snapshot it takes of from, the take, and then the walks, the decision and
the skeleton beside it.
Every document is written to a sibling
.Pa <file>.tmp
in the destination's own directory and renamed over the destination, which
cannot cross a filesystem and so cannot be anywhere else; a reader finds
the document whole or as it was and never half of either.
A write that fails takes its own
.Pa .tmp
away, and a write that succeeds leaves nothing behind.
A
.Pa .tmp
beside a
.Fl o
file is what a crash in the middle of one write leaves; it is the user's,
like the file it sits beside, and the tool never removes it.
.Pp
.Fl -restart
reads the header's
.Sy #take
line when it writes the resolution's skeleton again, so what comes back is
the document the run wrote and not a different one.
.Pp
There is no phase property at birth, and no
.Sy done
value at all: at done every one of these properties is taken off, so a
result carrying any of them is an open rebase and one carrying none has no
rebase, whatever its history.
A record with no phase is a rebase that was born and never decided \(em the
manifest it names is the header alone \(em which
.Fl -continue
and
.Fl -restart
refuse in those words, before they take the result over, and
.Fl -abort
takes away.
Every verb keys on the record and on the manifest it names.
A dataset carrying neither
.Sy zfs_rebase:manifest
nor
.Sy zfs_rebase:tag
locally is not a result of this tool and is never touched: the verbs refuse
it and exit 2.
.Sh HOLDS
One persistent hold per input snapshot \(em base, from and onto, three of
them \(em is taken at the start of a real run, under a tag unique to the run:
.Sy zr-
and twelve hex digits, recorded as
.Sy zfs_rebase:tag .
The holds carry no cleanup descriptor, so they outlive the process, and
they are released only at done or at
.Fl -abort .
While they are there,
.Nm zfs Cm destroy
refuses each of the three with
.Dq dataset is busy
and
.Nm zfs Cm holds
shows the tag.
A stranded rebase holds on purpose: it is continuable.
In the clone form onto's snapshot cannot be destroyed for a second reason,
which is ZFS's own rule about a clone's origin and not something this tool
arranges.
.Sh THE GATES
A rebase's progress is a sequence of gates, written to
.Sy zfs_rebase:phase
and nowhere else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
decided -> applying1 -> conflicts -> applying2 -> done
decided -> applying1 -> done               (no conflicts)
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy decided
goes down the moment the decision has been renamed over the header the run
was born with, and before the skeleton is written beside it: it is what
says the manifest is the decision and not that header.
.Pp
.Sy applying1
goes down immediately before the result stops being read-only, and the
clean actions of the manifest are applied under it whether the decision had
conflicts or not.
.Sy conflicts
is written after that apply verified itself \(em every action done or
blocked and every name outside the manifest as onto had it, the stage
putting back what was not \(em and is the hand-off.
The run wrote the resolution beside the manifest when it wrote the manifest:
one line per conflicted name, in the manifest's own tree grammar, each
carrying the conflict's group number and a choice of
.Sy \&- ,
.Sy keep ,
.Sy onto
or
.Sy from .
Lines are never added by hand and never removed; answering a conflict
changes its choice, and the document is complete when no
.Sy \&-
is left.
.Pp
The gate keys on that completeness and on a command that says to go on,
and on nothing else \(em not on the file being there, which it always is,
since the tool wrote it.
Only the person who answered the conflicts can say they are answered, and
the command is how they say it: a
.Fl -continue
over a complete document passes the gate, and one over a document with a
.Sy \&-
left in it prints how many names are unanswered and stops.
A fresh run is such a command too.
Where its own skeleton comes out complete \(em which is what
.Fl -take-onto
and
.Fl -take-from
make it, and what a
.Fl -restart
under one of them makes it again \(em the run hands the result back, tears
itself down and goes on through
.Sy applying2
to
.Sy done
in the same process, by the one code path a
.Fl -continue
uses.
.Fl -no-merge
holds it at the gate instead, complete or not, and leaves the gate where
it is for the next
.Fl -continue ;
past the gate the flag is refused, since there is nothing left for it to
stop.
.Fl -interactive
opens a child on the resolution there instead \(em an editor named with
.Fl i Ar command ,
or the built-in picker \(em and reads the document back when it exits 0;
anything else leaves the gate standing.
.Sy applying2
carries the choices out.
.Sy done
is no phase and is never written: once the result has verified and is
read-only again it is handed back \(em home, or to the void \(em and then
the holds are given back and then every
.Sy zfs_rebase
property is taken off, in that order, since the tag is the only handle on
those holds.
A kill between the two leaves a record whose holds are already released,
which
.Fl -continue
and
.Fl -abort
both take in their stride.
.Pp
What a kill leaves is the last gate reached and nothing else.
There is no phase at all until the decision, and a stop writes none:
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGHUP
stop at the next action and put readonly back, leaving the state at the
gate;
.Dv SIGKILL
leaves the same gate with readonly off, which
.Fl -continue
restores.
A stop at the last gate leaves the phase of the stage that ran before it,
and the
.Fl -continue
that follows redoes that stage, which is idempotent, and reaches done.
.Fl -continue
resumes from the gate its record names, and does the same thing whichever
kind of stop left it:
.Bl -tag -width "applying2" -compact
.It no state
start at
.Sy applying1 :
applying nothing again is what an idempotent apply does over a tree nothing
was applied to
.It Sy applying1
apply the manifest again and go on
.It Sy conflicts
check first, write the drift found into the resolution and put back any
conflict line the manifest marks that it no longer has, then go on when
the resolution is complete, else say how many of its names are
still unanswered and exit 1; with
.Fl -no-merge ,
stop there whatever it says
.It Sy applying2
carry the choices out again and go on
.El
.Pp
.Fl -no-merge
is refused at
.Sy applying2 :
it is past the merge.
A rebase that reached done has no record, so no verb resumes it, and
.Fl -verify
is the only one that has anything to say about it.
.Sh THE CHECKS
One verify at every gate, on a schedule no flag changes and none can skip.
The expected tree is onto's names with the manifest's actions applied and
the resolution's choices after them; the result is held against it on two
axes, the names and their content, and what a check finds is drift.
.Bl -tag -width "end of applying1" -compact
.It end of applying1
fixed, by the stage's own self-check.
Up to the conflicts gate the result is the run's own, so a name that is not
what the expected tree says is a stray: every clean action that did not land
is made again and every name no action spoke for is put back as onto had it.
This is the one fix in the tool.
.It entering conflicts
written into the resolution, printed, and never fixed.
Every
.Fl -continue
that arrives at that gate checks first: each name the result no longer holds
as onto had it becomes a line with the choice
.Sy keep ,
which the person answering the conflicts can leave, change to
.Sy onto
to have the name put back as onto had it, or change to
.Sy from
to have the manifest's own action made again.
From this gate on the tree is being edited by hand and nothing can tell that
work from a stray, so nothing here touches it.
.It the done gate
reported, and the exit status is 3; the gate is passed all the same.
.Sy done
is no phase and is never written: what says a rebase reached it is
that the result carries no record.
What was found is written into the resolution with the choice
.Sy \- ,
which is the record of it: a choice that was not carried out set back to
that, a name outside the manifest that drifted taken as a drift line, and a
conflict line the manifest marks that the document no longer has put back.
The check is made by whichever invocation reaches that gate, the fresh run's
own or a
.Fl -continue Ns 's,
and
.Fl -quiet
silences its report and nothing else.
.It a settled result
reported, and the exit status is 3, and nothing is written.
The
.Fl -verify
verb given the manifest a
.Fl -manifest
run left behind makes that same check over again, with no gate to make it
at, against the identity the header wrote down.
.El
.Pp
.Sy done
never blocks on drift.
What the last check finds is said and carried out in the exit status, and
the gate is passed regardless: the record cleared, the result settled and
the run directory taken away, exactly as on a clean pass.
A rebase that could not be closed because somebody edited a file in it would
be a rebase nothing could ever end.
A check that cannot be made at all is the other thing \(em that is the tool
failing, not the tree drifting \(em and the gate is not passed, so a
.Fl -continue
can try again.
.Pp
The
.Fl -verify
verb is the same check asked for on its own, at whatever gate the rebase is
waiting and after it is over: it reports and writes nothing at all, and
moves nothing that is where it should be.
.Pp
From the
.Sy conflicts
gate on the resolution is the authority.
Every line is carried out by its path and its choice, whoever wrote it: a
line the tool wrote, a line the person changed and a line the person added
alike, and a conflict line added by hand for a name the manifest never
marked has its group number ignored, since it belongs to no group.
.Sy applying2
leaves every
.Sy keep
line exactly as it is and carries out every
.Sy onto
and every
.Sy from
one, and it touches a name the document speaks for by no other means: a
conflict mark in the manifest is a line the apply skips.
What a hand edit cannot do is take a conflict away: a conflict line the
manifest marks that the document no longer has is put back \(em at the
.Sy conflicts
gate with the take mode's answer,
.Sy onto
under
.Fl -take-onto ,
.Sy from
under
.Fl -take-from ,
and
.Sy \-
where the run was given neither; and at the done gate with
.Sy \- ,
since by then the document is a record and not a question.
Two lines for one name are refused when the file is read: they are two
instructions for one object.
The verdict of every check then reads two documents and nothing else: an
action of the manifest not done, or a line of the resolution not done, is
drift.
.Sh FILES
Each run keeps its own directory, 0700 throughout, named for the dataset
carrying the record with its slashes kept as directories.
It is born at the start of the run and gone at done:
.Bl -tag -width "/var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/resolution" -compact
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/mnt
the private mount: where the result lives for the whole of the rebase, in
both forms, put there with
.Fn zfs_mount_at
and never through the
.Sy mountpoint
property
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/manifest
the manifest, unless
.Fl o
named somewhere else
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/resolution
the resolution, beside the manifest
.El
.Pp
Making the directory is the lock: a leaf that is there already is another
run of the same result, and the run is refused; where that result carries
no record the directory is what a crash left before the record, and
.Fl -abort
removes it.
Removing it at done is what frees the name again.
At done the run unlinks the two documents it wrote into that directory,
and then
.Pa mnt ,
the directory and every empty parent up to
.Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase
are removed by rmdir, never recursively, so a directory another run shares
simply stays.
This is the same in both forms and whichever invocation reaches done, the
fresh run's own or a
.Fl -continue Ns 's .
A failure to remove is printed and changes nothing else: the rebase is
done.
What a rebase that reached done leaves is the result \(em the clone,
unmounted, to be placed, or the dataset back at home with its
before-image beside it \(em and a
.Fl o
pair if one was asked for.
Nothing else.
.Pp
With
.Fl o Ar FILE
the manifest is
.Ar FILE
and the resolution is
.Ar FILE Ns .resolution ,
beside it.
The record names the manifest and the resolution is beside it by that
rule, so every verb finds both and never by guessing a path.
A pair named that way is the user's: the tool records it and never
removes it, at done or at
.Fl -abort ,
where its own two go with the run directory.
.Pp
Not
.Pa /var/run :
the system's cleanvar deletes every file there at boot, and a rebase
stopped at conflicts can outlast a reboot.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "0" -compact
.It 0
the rebase is done
.It 1
it stopped at conflicts
.It 2
it was refused before anything was touched
.It 3
something failed part way, or a check found drift
.El
.Pp
The last two are told apart by what is left behind.
A rebase whose final check found drift has reached
.Sy done
all the same and carries no record any more
.Pq Sx THE CHECKS ;
one that failed part way is still there, for a
.Fl -continue
to take on or an
.Fl -abort
to take away.
The
.Fl -verify
verb exits 0 when nothing has drifted and 3 when something has: an action
pending or drifted, a line of the resolution pending or drifted, or a name
the manifest never spoke for that the result no longer holds as onto had it.
Blocked and unchecked are states and not faults, and a keep is never
compared.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Rebase a topic snapshot onto the current main, in the clone form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -v --from tank/topic@work --onto tank/main@current \e
    --result tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
While the rebase is open the result is a read-only clone at
.Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/tank/rebased/mnt ,
and at done it is unmounted with its
.Sy mountpoint
property still
.Sy none ,
which the tool's last line says.
It never promotes it either: placing and adopting it are yours to do by
hand, when you have looked at it and are satisfied.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs promote tank/rebased
zfs rename tank/main tank/main.old
zfs rename tank/rebased tank/main
zfs inherit mountpoint tank/main
zfs inherit readonly tank/main
.Ed
.Pp
The same rebase in the dataset form, which writes into
.Pa tank/main
itself and keeps
.Pa tank/main@pre
as the before-image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -v --from tank/topic --onto tank/main --result pre
.Ed
.Pp
A run that was killed part way is a rebase, not a leftover.
Look at the gate it left and take it on from there:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs get -s local -H -o value zfs_rebase:phase tank/rebased
zfs_rebase --continue tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
The identifier is the rebase's name, so the last part of it does as
well where nothing else answers to it, and so does the manifest's
path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase --continue rebased
zfs_rebase --verify /var/db/zfs_rebase/tank/rebased/manifest
.Ed
.Pp
In the dataset form the rebase is named by the pre-apply snapshot the
run was given:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase --continue pre
zfs_rebase --verify tank/main@pre
.Ed
.Pp
Or take the whole thing away again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase --abort tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
A rebase with no human in it: every conflict answered onto in advance, so
that the run passes its own
.Sy conflicts
gate and reaches
.Sy done
in one process, and the same command written in letters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase --take-onto --from tank/topic@work \e
    --onto tank/main@current --result tank/rebased
zfs_rebase -O -f tank/topic@work -t tank/main@current \e
    -r tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
With
.Fl -no-merge
the same run stops at the gate with the document already answered, for a
script that means to edit it before the merge; the
.Fl -continue
after it passes the gate.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-hold 8 ,
.Xr zfs-promote 8 ,
.Xr zfs-rollback 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The tool never promotes.
The clone form's result is a read-only clone with no mountpoint of its own,
mounted at a directory of the run's choosing while the rebase is open and
unmounted at done, and placing, promoting, renaming or inheriting it is the
user's work, deliberately.
.Pp
Conflict resolution is not part of this tool.
A run with conflicts applies its clean actions, writes the resolution as a
skeleton of unanswered choices and stops at the
.Sy conflicts
gate; reading the conflicts back and presenting them for answering belong to
a separate conflict manager.
What this tool offers in its place is a blanket answer:
.Fl -take-onto
and
.Fl -take-from
write the skeleton answered one way for every conflict, which is a complete
document, so such a run goes to
.Sy done
without stopping.
That is a policy, not a merge, and it is meant for a script that knows what
it wants; the flags are not a substitute for looking at the conflicts.
.Pp
A verify of a rebase in flight is best effort.
Each input is looked for by name and then by guid across the pool, which is
what survives a rename or a promote, and what cannot be found is reported
as unchecked rather than guessed at.
A verify after done is not: the header is the whole of what a settled rebase
wrote down about itself, so each input must be there under the name it kept
and with the guid it kept, and anything else stops the check.
The one exception is a side given as a dataset: the snapshot the tool took
of it is gone at done by design, so the actions that would have to read it
are unchecked rather than a loss, and cannot be redone afterwards.
After done, drift on a live dataset is indistinguishable from new work,
which is why nothing past the
.Sy applying1
stage puts anything back:
.Fl -verify
says what it sees and the answer to it is yours.
